<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

// Help texts of the System status tab (lib/help/system_status.php). Split out of
// help.php, which had grown past the 400-line module budget: the tab already had
// its own renderer, so its strings get their own module rather than an arbitrary
// cut through one catalog. Keys are reached as __t('help_system_status.<key>').

return [
    'system_status_roles_heading' => 'Settings, credentials and system status',
    'system_status_roles_p1' => 'Settings control global behaviour, Credentials stores encrypted Ansible and ESXi accounts, and System status shows observed results plus authorised diagnostic and repair actions.',
    'system_status_single_site' => 'VirtuSphere connects exactly one MECM site. Several IP allowlist entries let several network addresses reach the same machine API; they do not create multi-MECM.',
    'system_status_signals_heading' => 'Reading result reports and the site state',
    'system_status_signal_heartbeat' => 'A current result report proves that exactly this scheduled task ran on the MECM server and reported its outcome to the portal. It says nothing about the other tasks; each one reports separately.',
    'system_status_signal_combination' => 'The integration (data flow) and the MECM site state are separate: the data flow can be green while MECM itself reports a warning, and a failed sync does not mean MECM is critical. Read the two badges separately.',
    'logs_heading' => 'Logs & troubleshooting',
    'logs_p1' => 'There are two kinds of logs. The deploy log (the "Log" link on each job under "Deploy") shows the output of the running Ansible job in real time; this is where to look when a deployment fails. The audit log under "Logs" (administrators only) records who changed what in the system and when.',
    'logs_p2' => 'The audit log is split into four sections (tabs at the top) so it is not one undifferentiated table: "Security" (sign-in & access, users, credentials), "Resources" (missions, VMs, VLANs, operating systems), "Deployment" (deploy, MECM integration) and "System & integrations" (system, settings, legacy API). Within a section you can further full-text search and filter by IP and by an individual category. The "MECM integration" category deliberately contains only significant events: a monitored source going down or coming back, automatically relinked package assignments and rejected sync runs. Individual heartbeats are not logged, otherwise the log would become unreadable within hours.',
    'logs_p2b' => 'The "Security" section records who has and had access to the system: successful and failed sign-ins, automatic account and IP locks, sign-outs, expired sessions, password changes, and refused requests (missing permission or invalid CSRF token). Change entries name the changed fields with their old and new value; secrets and notes only show as "changed". Data egress is recorded too: the JSON export of a mission and the CSV list exports. Deploy jobs log their outcome (succeeded or failed with the reason) in addition to being queued.',
    'logs_p3' => 'Cleanup is automatic via the maintenance service: security logs (sign-in, users, credentials) are kept for :security_days days, all other portal log entries for :other_days days, the sign-in attempt counter for :attempt_days days, client phase reports for :client_days days, retired packages without VM assignments for :package_days days. Nobody has to delete logs by hand.',
    'logs_p4' => 'Deploy-job output is cleaned up too, but only :log_days days after the job has finished. A running job never loses a line. Mission jobs stay in the list with their status and error message; opening their log later shows a note instead of an empty table. Mission-less ESXi inventory jobs are removed entirely after :system_days days. Until then, their System-status card links the latest completed job directly; afterwards an old failure shows a retention note instead of a dead link.',
    'firstaid_heading' => 'First steps when something fails',
    'firstaid_p1' => 'Whatever is stuck, the order is always the same. This is how to find out within minutes which component is affected:',
    'firstaid_step1' => 'Open System status: is any source at "Delayed" or "Down"? If so, the matching action hint is shown right next to it; the typical diagnoses further down on this page help as well.',
    'firstaid_step2' => 'If a deployment failed: under "Deploy", click "Log" on the affected job. It contains the complete Ansible output with the actual error message.',
    'firstaid_step3' => 'If a VM is stuck in the Windows installation (stage 4/5 or 5/5): open the "Client phases" section on the "Edit VM" page. It shows which step ran last and whether one of them failed.',
    'firstaid_step4' => 'If that is not enough: the operations handbook is in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/mecm-integration.md. It describes the log files on the MECM server and the clients, plus the most common cases step by step.',
    'system_status_heading' => 'System status: health at a glance',
    'system_status_p1' => 'VirtuSphere only works together with scripts running on the MECM server. System status is the first place to look when something does not arrive: it separates MECM synchronisation, MECM site state, Ansible test, ESXi inventory and internal services, with an action hint for problematic states.',
    'system_status_p2' => 'The page is deliberately visible to every signed-in user, not just administrators. The first diagnosis therefore needs no admin rights: anyone can check whether a source is at "Down" before reporting a problem.',
    'system_status_p3' => 'The page is a snapshot of the moment it loaded and does not refresh itself. "View generated" names that moment, "Refresh status" fetches a new one. A window left open will not turn green once the problem is fixed.',
    'system_status_sources_heading' => 'What is monitored?',
    'system_status_source_1' => 'MECM device sync. Scheduled task "VirtuSphere MECM Devices Sync", script mecm_new-device-sync.ps1. It asks the portal for VMs whose MAC address is known, creates them as devices in MECM and assigns them to their mission collection. If the task is not running, every new VM stays stuck at stage 3/5: the MAC has been reported, but MECM never learns about the VM.',
    'system_status_source_2' => 'MECM package sync. Scheduled task "VirtuSphere MECM Packages Sync", script mecm_Packages-TaskSeq-sync.ps1. It reads the application catalog and the Task Sequences out of MECM and reports them to the portal, which is where the "Packages" and "Operating Systems" pages come from. If the task is not running, the selection goes stale silently: new packages are missing, retired ones stay.',
    'system_status_source_3' => 'MECM package import (autoimporter). Scheduled task "VirtuSphere MECM Package Import", script mecm_autoimporter.ps1. It looks into the package folder on the MECM server and turns every new subfolder holding a config.json into a MECM application including collection and deployment. If the task is not running, dropped packages simply sit there and appear neither in MECM nor in the portal.',
    'system_status_source_4' => 'MECM site health: the official MECM-reported site state (green, yellow, red). A dedicated SYSTEM task on the MECM server queries SMS_SummarizerSiteStatus and reports the state to the portal.',
    'system_status_source_5' => 'Maintenance service (portal). Not a scheduled task on the MECM server but a container of its own on the portal server, running continuously. It handles the cleanup work and the ESXi inventory scheduling and reports itself while doing so; its rhythm is shown in the row under "Interval". If it stops, that cleanup and scheduling work is left undone, while the MECM tasks keep running unaffected.',
    'system_status_status_heading' => 'Reading the status display',
    'system_status_status_p1' => 'The MECM sources are PowerShell scripts running as scheduled tasks in Windows Task Scheduler on the MECM server. After each run they send a short report to the portal; the technical term for such a report is "heartbeat". The portal polls nothing, it only waits. If no report arrives for longer than the expected rhythm, the status changes. Every state is shown with text and colour:',
    'system_status_status_p2' => 'Every row shows the same fields in the same order, even where a source never reported one of them. A dash instead of a value therefore means exactly one thing: nothing is stored for this field. It does not say the event never happened; a task that has never failed and one whose failure time never arrived look the same here. A row from an older script has no success time, only its last report; that sits in the same place under a different name.',
    // The expandable block under the counters. Device-sync and the autoimporter
    // used to send nothing there, so "data warnings: 3" named not a single VM.
    'system_status_status_p3' => 'When a row counts data warnings or failures, "Technical details" holds a cause line: one short code per case, followed by "target=" with the VM or folder name and, where an assignment is involved, "collection=" with the collection. For example "collection_missing target=WEB01 collection=Firefox-115.0". That finds the affected VM without searching the log on the MECM server. The list is capped and says at the end how many cases are not shown. The counters themselves always describe the last run only: a clean run resets them, so read the cause line while it is there.',
    'system_status_work_heading' => 'Working with the status hints',
    'system_status_work_0' => 'If neither a synchronisation task nor the site-health reporter has ever reported, MECM simply is not connected yet. The rows then carry no hint texts, because there is nothing to repair that does not exist yet; instead the section names the next step once.',
    'system_status_work_1' => 'On "Delayed" or "Down", first read the hint text right next to the row. It states the most likely cause and the next step in plain language; nobody has to memorise what each source means.',
    'system_status_work_2' => 'Carry out the step or hand it over precisely: some measures (starting scheduled tasks on the MECM server, changing settings) need admin or server access. In that case report the affected row together with its hint text to the responsible person; that saves them half the diagnosis.',
    'system_status_work_3' => 'After the fix, wait briefly: every source reports on its fixed schedule ("Interval" in the row). The row only returns to "OK" with the next heartbeat, not immediately after the intervention. When it turns "OK" the action hint disappears with it, because the hint is a repair instruction, not a description.',
    'system_status_work_4' => 'If the row stays at "Down" anyway: continue with the typical diagnoses below and then consult the operations handbook in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/mecm-integration.md.',
    'system_status_fix_heading' => 'Typical diagnoses',
    'system_status_fix_1' => 'Sync sources at "Down", but "MECM site" at "OK" (green): MECM itself is running, only the scheduled tasks are not reporting. Open Task Scheduler on the MECM server and start the tasks "VirtuSphere MECM Devices Sync", "VirtuSphere MECM Packages Sync" or "VirtuSphere MECM Package Import".',
    'system_status_fix_2' => 'MECM site at "Down" (red): MECM itself reports the site state as critical. Check the details in the MECM console under Monitoring, System Status. A grey provider fault only means the site-health reporter could not query the SMS provider, not that MECM is critical.',
    'system_status_fix_3' => '"Maintenance service" at "Down": the internal service of the portal has stopped. On the portal server (Ubuntu), check "docker compose ps" in the project directory and restart with "docker compose up -d maintenance-worker".',
    'system_status_fix_4' => 'For deeper cases (log files on the MECM server, script edge cases) there is an operations handbook in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/mecm-integration.md.',
    'mecmfolders_heading' => 'Folder structure in the MECM console: what lives where?',
    'mecmfolders_p1' => 'Everything VirtuSphere creates lives in three fixed folders in the MECM console. This keeps it cleanly separated from manually maintained MECM objects, and new staff can find their way around immediately:',
    'mecmfolders_item1' => 'VirtuSphere_OS (under Assets and Compliance → Device Collections): one collection per task sequence, with the same name. VMs receive their operating system through these; created by the device sync.',
    'mecmfolders_item2' => 'VirtuSphere_Missions (also under Device Collections): one collection per mission, named exactly like the mission in the portal; created by the device sync.',
    'mecmfolders_item3' => 'VirtuSphere_Applications: exists twice, once under Device Collections (one collection "Name-Version" per package) and once under Software Library → Applications (the applications themselves); populated by the autoimporter. Important: the package sync reads exactly this collections folder as its catalog source. Only collections inside this folder appear as packages in the portal.',
    'mecmfolders_p2' => 'All three folders are created by the scripts themselves when needed, nobody has to create them by hand (requires the MECM console version 2111 or later; on older versions create the folders manually once). The devices themselves land in "All Systems" without a dedicated folder and are only assigned to the collections.',
    'mecmfolders_p3' => 'For day-to-day work this means: a package whose collection sits outside VirtuSphere_Applications does not show up in the portal; after moving it into the folder it does. And please do not rename the folders or their contents, because the names are the link between the portal and MECM.',
    'clientphases_heading' => 'Client phases: watching the Windows installation',
    'clientphases_p1' => 'From stage 4/5 on it is no longer the portal doing the work but the VM itself: it PXE-boots, installs Windows, and small scripts on the freshly installed system report their progress back. The "Client phases" section on the "Edit VM" page shows four steps that always run in this order:',
    'clientphases_step1' => 'getinfo: the client fetches its device information (including network interfaces) from the portal by MAC and stores it locally in the registry; the following phases (hostname, staticip) read their target values from there. This is why getinfo must always run first.',
    'clientphases_step2' => 'hostname: the computer is renamed to the hostname stored in the portal and then reboots.',
    'clientphases_step3' => 'staticip: the network is switched to the target configuration; the VM often changes VLAN in the process and is briefly unreachable.',
    'clientphases_step4' => 'disks: additional disks are brought online (optional; VMs without extra disks skip this step).',
    'clientphases_p2' => 'Every phase reports "started", "finished" or "failed". The status "Executed, confirmation pending" appears when the start was reported but the completion report has been missing for :minutes minutes. This is usually harmless: after the rename the VM reboots, after the network switch it sits in a different VLAN; in both cases the completion report simply cannot reach the portal any more.',
    'clientphases_p3' => 'Only "Failed" with a detail text is a real error. In that case the script log directly on the affected VM under C:\\Program Files\\VirtuSphere\\Logs helps.',
    'esxi_inv_heading' => 'ESXi inventory',
    'esxi_inv_p1' => 'The portal reads datacenters, datastores, portgroups and host capacity read-only from the registered ESXi credentials at a regular interval. The values are snapshots from the last fetch, not live monitoring. The interval is set under Settings (default :hours hours); a manual refresh and an immediate pull after adding a credential are also available.',
    'esxi_inv_p2' => 'If a fetch fails, an error category appears (unreachable, login, permissions, Ansible host, parsing). On a wrong login the automatic fetch pauses until the credential is changed (lockout protection). The cache is kept on errors; nothing is deleted.',
    'esxi_inv_cadence' => 'Under the timestamps of every inventory card a line says whether and how often these values renew themselves. Where it names a reason instead (automation off, no Ansible credential selected, credential paused), that is the reason stopping the fetch and the one to fix first: the others only take effect again after it.',
    'esxi_inv_rights_heading' => 'Required ESXi rights',
    'esxi_inv_rights_read' => 'Inventory (read-only): read access to datacenters, datastores, network/portgroups and host objects.',
    'esxi_inv_rights_deploy' => 'Deploy (playbooks): create VMs, use datastore space, assign networks, power operations, and disk/NIC configuration.',
    'esxi_inv_p3' => 'If a right is missing, the "insufficient permissions" category appears; then extend the account\'s ESXi role. Details are in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/esxi-inventory.md.',
    'esxi_inv_deviation' => 'Values that are not in the current inventory show up as a deviation (the "Deviations" section on System status, a badge on the mission list, a hint when deploying). While no ESXi inventory exists there is nothing to compare against, so the section reports "Not checked" instead of a green zero. Deviations of a template are marked as such, because they only become a problem once a mission is created from it. Missions, per-VM overrides and the VLANs of the network interfaces are checked; the badge also lights up when only one VM of the mission is affected. This blocks nothing; it just flags a value to check. If a VLAN was renamed on ESXi, the guided mass reassignment moves all assignments of one VLAN name to an active entry; it affects portal records only, never ESXi. The form only appears when there is an actual VLAN deviation.',
    'esxi_inv_refresh_pause' => 'The "Refresh all" button skips paused credentials (lockout protection) and says so in the notice. A paused credential can still be retried deliberately via its own refresh button, or reactivated by saving the credential.',
    'esxi_inv_ampel_heading' => 'Traffic light of the inventory fetch',
    'esxi_inv_ampel_p1' => 'The traffic light shows the health of the fetch only. Red: login paused or at least :streak consecutive failures. Yellow: last fetch failed, never succeeded, or the last success is older than :factor times the interval. Green: last fetch successful and current. With interval 0 (automation off) the staleness warning is dropped; only real failures colour the light. Host properties such as a free licence or an HA cluster do not colour the light; they appear as their own badges (see "Host notes"). The same traffic light appears on the credentials page and as the "Hypervisor" dashboard tile; they cannot drift apart.',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_heading' => 'VLAN catalog: presence and VLAN ID',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_presence' => 'The "On ESXi" column shows on how many successfully fetched hosts a portgroup exists. Partial presence (say on 3 of 7 hosts) is marked yellow and names the missing hosts: a deploy to one of those hosts would not find the portgroup.',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_vlanid' => 'The "VLAN ID" column compares the IDs of same-named portgroups across hosts. Different IDs are marked red, because the same name with another ID means another network. Trunks/ranges are not compared; older fetches without ID data show a placeholder until the next fetch.',
    'esxi_inv_templates' => 'The deviation report also shows templates (marked "Template"): a stale VLAN living only in a template would otherwise silently propagate into every mission created from it. Clicking a VLAN deviation value prefills the mass reassignment.',
    'esxi_test_heading' => 'Testing a credential',
    'esxi_test_p1' => 'The portal never talks to ESXi directly. For an ESXi account, "Start inventory pull" on the Credentials page queues the same read-only pull that saving the credential triggers: over the Ansible host, the exact path a deploy takes. The first message only reports the queue or an already-open job.',
    'esxi_test_p2' => 'The result then lives on this page as a traffic light, permanently, rather than as a one-off message. Clicking the status mark under Credentials jumps to the exact entry, highlights it and keeps it fully visible below the sticky topbar. Queued and running jobs link directly to their job log there, as does the latest completed pull – including a success, so partial queries can be checked. Once retention removes it, an old failure shows a note instead of a dead link; a new pull creates a new log.',
    'esxi_test_ansible_heading' => 'Ansible credential: what the test checks',
    'esxi_test_ansible_p1' => 'An Ansible credential is tested immediately and in full, because that path runs from the portal itself: SSH login, then the toolchain on the host (ansible-playbook, python3, the pyvmomi and requests Python modules and the community.vmware collection), a real SFTP write into /tmp and, if an API base URL is set, whether the host can reach the portal (health.php) and whether its IP is on the machine API IP allowlist. A missing allowlist entry is reported as a warning, not a failure: the credential works, but a deploy\'s MAC upload would be rejected. When something fails, the message names the component that broke instead of a bare exit code; the raw output sits behind "technical details".',
    'esxi_test_ansible_p2' => 'The result stays visible: as a status badge in the "Status" column on the Credentials page and as its own row in the "Ansible host" section of System status, each with the time of the last test. Unlike the ESXi pull, the Ansible test runs only on click, not on a schedule, so the badge ages out into "Unconfirmed" once the result is too old to prove anything.',
    'esxi_test_ansible_p3' => 'When the credential is edited, the portal discards the stored result and the badge returns to "Not tested": the old result proved the old host and the old account, not the new values. Test once again after every change.',
    'esxi_cap_heading' => 'Reading the host notes',
    'esxi_cap_p1' => 'After a successful pull the portal shows what the host is (product, version, licence, direct or through vCenter) and what it is doing. These notes do not say something is broken; they say what the host can do:',
    'esxi_cap_unknown' => 'When a fact is unknown, no note is shown at all. It is never read as "everything is fine". A job is only refused when the fact comes from a fresh successful pull; stale facts merely warn and let the job run.',
    'esxi_inv_host_facts' => 'Under "Open inventory details" every host row also names its core count. If the host clock was more than :minutes minutes away from portal time at the moment of the pull, a warning appears there: a badly set ESXi clock can break the domain join of freshly created VMs (Kerberos tolerates only a few minutes). Fix it with NTP on the ESXi host, not in the portal.',
    'esxi_cause_heading' => 'Causes of a failed pull',
    'esxi_cause_p1' => 'When a pull fails, the portal maps the cause to a fixed category. While the job is retained, the failure alert links directly to its job log with the original technical text. After the retention window the dead link is removed; start a new pull then.',
    'esxi_cause_th_cause' => 'Category',
    'esxi_cause_th_meaning' => 'Meaning',
    'esxi_cause_th_fix' => 'What to do',
    'esxi_cause_host_placeholder' => 'the host',
    'esxi_cause_fix_dns' => 'Check the hostname in the credential, plus the DNS and search domain of the portal container.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_unreachable' => 'Check network, firewall and port. The pull retries automatically at the next interval; the existing cache is kept.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_certificate' => 'Under Credentials, check the CA bundle or server certificate and the ESXi host name, then run the strict connection test again.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_tls' => 'Check the TLS version, HTTPS port and peer. Certificate failures are reported separately as certificate.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_auth' => 'Correct the username and secret. The automatic pull pauses until the credential changes, so the ESXi account cannot be locked out.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_authz' => 'Extend the role of the ESXi account. Read rights are enough for the inventory, but not for deploying or autostart.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_http' => 'No longer occurs for ESXi pulls. If the category shows on an old record, a fresh pull clears it.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_ssh' => 'Check the Ansible credential and the Ansible host, exactly as for a failed deploy.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_worker' => 'Check the deploy worker on System status and restart the service. The job log shows after which missing heartbeat the job was stopped.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_parse' => 'The cause is unknown, so read the job log: it carries the playbook output verbatim. Often the Ansible collection version does not match the host.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_config' => 'The fetch failed before it contacted the host. Check host, username and type in the credential; if the job log names a missing file or module, it is missing on the Ansible host, not on ESXi.',
    'esxi_inv_zero_heading' => 'Green pull, and still a zero',
    'esxi_inv_zero_p1' => 'A pull consists of several separate queries: datacenters, datastores, standard portgroups, distributed portgroups and host data. If one of them returns nothing, the pull still finishes cleanly; the card shows a 0 for that kind and the traffic light stays green, because it only answers whether the pull ran. A 0 next to filled numbers is therefore not a contradiction, it is the hint to look at exactly one query.',
    'esxi_inv_zero_p2' => 'What matters is what the credential points at. Standard portgroups and host data are read from the ESXi host itself; if the credential points at a vCenter, exactly those two stay empty while datacenters and datastores arrive. Distributed portgroups are the other way round and exist only under vCenter: on a single ESXi host their 0 is the normal case. For a complete inventory, a credential pointing at the ESXi host is the verified way.',
    'esxi_inv_zero_p3' => 'Nobody has to guess: every pull records which of its queries answered. The job log of the job carries an "Inventory queries" line right below the counts. Either it reports that every query answered ("all ... answered"), which means every count is trustworthy and a 0 is a real 0. Or it names each silent query with one of two words: "rejected" means the query was refused, often before it ever reached ESXi, and the message behind it is the module\'s own; "skipped" means the playbook had nothing to ask, for instance distributed portgroups without a known datacenter. Queries that failed the same way are named together in front of one message.',
    'esxi_inv_zero_p4' => 'Nothing is lost in the meantime: an empty partial result leaves the existing entries of that kind untouched, and the VLAN catalog only retires a portgroup once a pull demonstrably stops reporting it. If the "Inventory queries" line is missing entirely, the pull came from an older state that could not yet say anything about its completeness; a fresh pull fixes that.',
    'esxi_inv_zero_p5' => 'Right below it sits the "Datastore health" line. It states for how many datastores the pull delivered accessibility and maintenance mode at all, and then names the ones in maintenance or not reachable. The good case is stated too, for the same reason as with the queries: a fleet with nothing in maintenance and a field the pull no longer hits would otherwise look identical. A datastore in maintenance is marked in the selection lists, and its free space counts as unknown everywhere rather than as available.',
];
